# Oblisk's PAM stack, for the lock screen (docs/adr/0052) and polkit authentication
# (docs/adr/0028). Install to /etc/pam.d/oblisk.
#
# Until this file exists the worker authenticates against "login" instead, which works but is the
# console-login stack and does considerably more than an unlock needs: pam_shells, pam_nologin,
# pam_access, pam_time, pam_lastlog2, pam_motd, pam_mail, pam_loginuid, pam_keyinit and
# pam_systemd all sit on the path between a typed password and an unlocked screen.
#
# Two of those are not merely surplus, they are ways to be locked out of your own session:
# pam_nologin refuses authentication outright while /etc/nologin exists, and pam_shells refuses
# an account whose shell is not in /etc/shells. Neither has anything to say about whether the
# person at the keyboard is the person who locked the screen.
#
# `system-auth` rather than a hand-written list, so the machine's real password policy is what
# answers -- pam_faillock, pam_unix and pam_systemd_home included, whatever the admin has set.
# Only auth and account: `run_conversation` calls `authenticate` then `account_management` and
# opens no session, so a session or password chain here would be dead configuration.
auth      include   system-auth
account   include   system-auth
